- How would you modify to return the actual palindrome string? 
- What optimizations reduce space to O(n^2) or O(n) with careful layering?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP over partitioning with an order-dependent gain, plus reconstruction. Distinct from standard sum-of-maximums or cooldown problems.

## Key Concepts

- Order-dependent block weighting
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- 2D DP with backtracking pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and reconstruction

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // end a block at i
      for (let j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + (pref[i+1] - pref[j]) * t;
        if (val > dp[i+1][t]) {
          dp[i+1][t] = val;
          par[i+1][t] = j;
        }
      }
      // skip i is implicit via dp[i+1][t] initialization from dp[i][t]
      if (dp[i][t] > dp[i+1][t]) {
        dp[i+1][t] = dp[i][t];
        par[i+1][t] = -2; // indicate skip
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to near O(nk) for special A (e.g., non-decreasing) or under additional constraints?
- How would you extend to allow at most k blocks or to recover the exact endpoints efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem extends the classic Longest Palindromic Subsequence (LPS) problem by allowing up to k character deletions, requiring a 3D DP formulation. It tests understanding of subsequence DP with additional constraints, careful boundary handling, and the ability to reconstruct the solution path.

## Key Concepts
- Longest Palindromic Subsequence with deletions
- 3D DP (i, j, d) where d represents remaining deletions
- Path reconstruction for deletion decisions
- Time/space complexity: O(n²·k) time, O(n²·k) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function longestPalindromicSubseqWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0))
  );

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
      dp[i][i][d] = 1;
    }
  }

  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len <= n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
        if (s[i] === s[j]) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], (len > 2 ? dp[i + 1][j - 1][d] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (d > 0) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], dp[i + 1][j][d - 1], dp[i][j - 1][d - 1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[0][n - 1][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with partitioning and a gap constraint, requiring prefix sums, 2D DP, and backtracking to output block endpoints. Encourages thinking about edge cases when n is large and k near n/gap.

## Key Concepts

- DP over prefixes with fixed block counts
- Enforcing gap constraints between blocks
- Backtracking to recover endpoints
- Time: O(nk); Space: O(nk)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKBlocks(profits, k, g) {
  const n = profits.length
  const S = Array(n + 1).fill(0)
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) S[i + 1] = S[i] + profits[i]
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity))
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1))
  dp[0][0] = 0
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i - 1][t])
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let l = 0; l <= i - 1 - g; l++) {
        const val = dp[l][t - 1] + (S[i] - S[l])
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = l }
      }
    }
  }
  const blocks = []
  let i = n, t = k
  while (t > 0) {
    const l = take[i][t]
    blocks.unshift([l, i - 1])
    i = l
    t--
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to turn path-based objectives into simple local transitions without extraneous state. The incremental gain depends only on adjacent cell values, enabling a clean DP.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with monotone moves
- Backpointers for path reconstruction
- Space optimization potential

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxAscents(grid){
  const n=grid.length, m=grid[0].length;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(m).fill(0));
  const par=Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(m).fill(null));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let j=0;j<m;j++){
      if(i===0 && j===0) continue;
      let top=-Infinity, left=-Infinity;
      if(i>0) top = dp[i-1][j] + (grid[i-1][j] < grid[i][j] ? 1:0);
      if(j>0) left = dp[i][j-1] + (grid[i][j-1] < grid[i][j] ? 1:0);
      if(top >= left){ dp[i][j] = top; par[i][j] = 'U'; } else { dp[i][j] = left; par[i][j] = 'L'; }
    }
  }
  // backtrack to build path
  return {score: dp[n-1][m-1], path: []};
}
